Subject: [PATCH] fix(header): my last fix traded an overflow for an overlap
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The header no longer runs off the right. It printed the brand on top of
"Services" and "Problems" on top of the search field instead. That is worse:
overflow loses something off the edge, overlap makes what is left unreadable.

WHAT I GOT WRONG

The shed rules were written behind `@media (max-width: 1439px)`, on the
assumption that a wider window gives the header more room. It does not.
`--shell-max` caps `.topbar-inner` at 1280px on a 4K monitor exactly as it does
on a laptop, so above 1439 nothing was shed and the same 1440px of content was
still being asked into 1192px. Below 1439 the shed made it fit, which is why
the first round measured clean and the bug only appeared on a big screen.

Then `min-width: 0` — correct, and necessary for the dropdown — let the nav
shrink, and its children spilled out over their neighbours instead of pushing
the row wide.

AND WHY MY OWN MEASUREMENT SAID IT WAS FINE

I measured `.topbar-inner`, and asked it for scrollWidth − width. A flex
container whose item has shrunk reports ZERO overflow: the item fits the line.
The item's contents do not fit the item, and that is a different box. Measured
at 1474px: brand over "Services" by 69px, "Problems" over the search field by
70px, and every overflow number on the row reading 0.

THE FIX

The shed is unconditional now, because the constraint is the shell and the
shell never grows. Two things go for good:

  Send photos   124px — it cannot fit a 1280px shell at ANY window size, so
                        there is no width at which showing it is correct.
  Search label   80px — the magnifier is the convention and ⌘K still works.

"Login" keeps its word: it is a client portal, not a universally understood
icon, and there is room for it.

Send photos does not simply vanish. It is the fastest route a visitor has to a
real answer, so it now leads the Services menu under "Start here", alongside
the free estimate — one click from every page, next to the six services rather
than competing with them for 124px of bar.

Verified with no overlap, no nav or strip overflow, chrome never covering
content, CTA on screen, both panels on screen and zero document overflow at:
320, 360, 390, 414, 430, 540, 768, 834, 1000, 1024, 1180, 1200, 1280, 1366,
1440, 1474, 1536, 1600, 1728, 1920, 2560 and 3840px.

audit-rendered.mjs LEARNS THE CHECK IT DID NOT HAVE

verify-chrome.mjs reads CSS and cannot see this: no declaration is wrong on its
own. Only a browser can say whether two boxes intersect, so the browser audit
now asks.

Two things had to be right for it to work:

  · LEAVES, NOT SIBLINGS. The first version compared each element with its own
    siblings and found nothing — the real collisions were .brand-lockup against
    a child of .topbar-nav, and "Problems" inside the nav against the search
    field inside .topbar-cta. Cousins, not siblings. A row is laid out from
    leaves across several containers, so leaves are what must not intersect.
  · A WIDE VIEWPORT. The audit's largest was 1280, and this bug only exists
    above 1439 — the widest windows shed the least and cram the most. Added
    desktop-1600. Without it the detector was correct and blind.

Validated both directions: with the defect reintroduced it names all three
collisions and their pixel counts; with the fix in place it reports none.

47 guards green. tsc unchanged.

